{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"rookie-fickle-hydra","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\n`rebar transition <id> in_progress closed --ref origin/main` resolves the ref MORE THAN ONCE\nduring a close. If `origin/main` advances between the verification step and the signing step,\ncompletion verification PASSES against the first sha and the signer then REFUSES to attest,\nbecause the state it is asked to sign has drifted from the state that was verified.\n\nThe close SUCCEEDS. The ticket reaches `closed` with `class` recorded and no error is printed.\nWhat is silently missing is the completion-verifier attestation.\n\n## Observed\n\nClosing `cutprice-untameable-stork` (326f-595a-088b-40a5):\n\n- verification ran against `6fe5c0d54574`\n- `origin/main` advanced to `d44bd253ee44` mid-run\n- verification PASSED; the signer refused the drifted state\n- result: `status=closed`, `close_class=preexisting`, **no completion-verifier signature**\n\nNothing in the output flags the gap. It is only visible if you go looking for the attestation.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe attestation is the durable evidence that a close was earned rather than asserted. A close\nthat passes verification but silently loses its signature is indistinguishable, after the fact,\nfrom one that was never verified — which is precisely the property the gate exists to provide.\n\nThe window is not narrow in practice: a completion-verified close takes 9-11 minutes, and on a\nrepo with active CI and multiple agents `origin/main` moves several times an hour. This session\nalone moved main a dozen times.\n\n## Mechanism (hypothesis — not yet proven)\n\nTwo independent resolutions of the same symbolic ref, with a multi-minute LLM verification\nbetween them. A single resolve-once-then-reuse would remove the race. I have NOT read the close\npath to confirm where the second resolution happens; whoever picks this up should.\n\n## Workaround, and why it is not a fix\n\nPass an immutable sha:\n\n    SHA=$(git rev-parse origin/main)\n    rebar transition <id> in_progress closed --class <c> --ref \"$SHA\"\n\nThat works, but it requires every caller to know the hazard. The documented guidance and the\ntool's own help present `--ref origin/main` as ordinary usage, and the CLI close gate's DEFAULT\n(worktree HEAD) has the same shape — a HEAD that moves mid-run would race identically.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A symbolic `--ref` is resolved ONCE and the resolved sha is used for both verification and\n      signing.\n- [ ] If the two ever disagree, the close FAILS loudly rather than succeeding unsigned.\n- [ ] A close that cannot obtain an attestation says so in its output, rather than reporting\n      success and leaving the gap for a reader to discover.\n- [ ] A regression test drives a close with the ref advancing mid-run and asserts the chosen\n      behaviour.\n\n## Found by\n\nA close performed during this session; the missing signature was noticed only because the\nclosing agent checked for it explicitly. Recorded because the failure is silent by construction.","id":"3e21-4c5b-3e0d-4e23","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["gates","close","attestation","race"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Close gate races on a moving --ref: verification passes at one sha, signing refuses at another"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787891914364322001,"uuid":"cd4a069a-f2d7-44f9-937f-c3c4b9f2640b"}